from review import card,save,DATE

i='TCS-3017';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Definition 1, p. 17:7 (ℓ₁ distance and success 3/4); Section 3.2.1, pp. 17:13–17:14, especially the question following Corollary 10.'
refs.extend([dict(id='streaming2019',title='Communication and Memory Efficient Testing of Discrete Distributions',authors='Ilias Diakonikolas; Themis Gouleakis; Daniel M. Kane; Sushrut Rao',year=2019,url='https://proceedings.mlr.press/v99/diakonikolas19a.html',locator='COLT 2019, full paper: Section 1.1, Appendix B.2 (state updates) and Appendix D (the o(log n)-memory question).'),dict(id='memory2022',title='On the Memory Complexity of Uniformity Testing',authors='Tomer Berg; Or Ordentlich; Ofer Shayevitz',year=2022,url='https://proceedings.mlr.press/v178/berg22a.html',locator='COLT 2022: Section 1, equations (1)–(6), Theorems 1–2 and the explicit comparison with the time-varying streaming model; Section 2.')])
save(i,dict(title='Sample complexity of uniformity testing with less than one stored sample',formal='Determine the minimum sample count q*(n,ε,m) for one-pass uniformity testing with m bits of persistent memory, including when this count is infinite, in the scarce-memory regime m=o(max{log₂ n,log₂(1/ε)}). Here n≥2 is an integer, 0<ε<1, and m≥1 is an integer. The tester receives independent samples from an unknown distribution P on [n], must accept P=Uₙ with probability at least 3/4, and must reject every P with ‖P−Uₙ‖₁≥ε with probability at least 3/4. The central case in the source is fixed ε and m=o(log n); the displayed wider regime is its explicit extension for small ε. Determine matching asymptotic upper and lower bounds up to universal constant factors in sample count throughout the stated regime.',definitions='Write [n]={1,…,n}, Uₙ(j)=1/n, and ‖P−Uₙ‖₁=Σⱼ₌₁ⁿ|P(j)−1/n|. A tester with horizon q is a layered randomized streaming machine: after t samples its state M_t lies in a set of at most 2^m possibilities. An initial state or initial state distribution is fixed independently of P. At step t its new state is chosen using only M_{t−1}, the current sample X_t and fresh independent randomness. The transition kernel may depend on t,n,ε,m,q, but not on P. After exactly q samples it outputs a Boolean decision from M_q, possibly with fresh randomness. Every item of retained information, including retained random bits, belongs to the state; past samples cannot be revisited. The current input symbol is available for the transition without first being copied into persistent memory. Transition tables, the layer index and computation between arrivals are not charged as extra memory; no uniform construction-time or running-time bound is imposed. This is the time-varying information-theoretic streaming convention, not a single fixed transition rule forced to store its own clock. Probability is over the independent sample stream and all tester randomness. q* is the least nonnegative integer q admitting such a tester, and is ∞ if no finite q works. The source’s little-o regime means parameter sequences with n→∞ and m/max{log₂ n,log₂(1/ε)}→0; constants in the requested sample bounds do not depend on those parameters. No correctness promise is imposed at intermediate distances 0<‖P−Uₙ‖₁<ε.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='Characterize exactly which parameter sequences in the stated scarce-memory regime permit finite-sample testing and give a sample bound with a matching lower bound up to constant factors there. Lower bounds must apply to all of the defined time-varying randomized streaming testers, not only collision-based algorithms or time-invariant machines.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Memory can be smaller than one alphabet symbol',text='Recording a single sample from an n-element domain takes about log₂ n bits. The question asks what testing remains possible when the tester cannot retain even one complete observation, and how much additional data can compensate for that restriction.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A distribution property rather than full estimation',text='The tester only distinguishes the uniform law from laws separated by a fixed ℓ₁ gap. It need not reconstruct individual probabilities, and its decision must work for every alternative distribution satisfying the promise.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Known collision bounds do not cover the unresolved regime',text='The 2021 construction processes batches of stored samples. Its discussion after Corollary 10 explicitly leaves scarce memory open. The printed bound must not be extrapolated below the memory needed for those stored samples merely by substituting a small m into its formula.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A later lower bound uses a different clock convention',text='Berg, Ordentlich and Shayevitz prove logarithmic-in-n memory lower bounds for fixed distance in a model with a time-invariant transition rule and asymptotic time-averaged error. They explicitly distinguish the earlier, more permissive time-varying streaming model. Their theorem therefore does not settle the finite-horizon question stated here.',citation='memory2022')],why='Would locate the statistical power of streaming computation below the memory needed to retain one observation and determine whether extra samples can compensate for this severe storage restriction.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='The original streaming work poses the existence of uniformity testers using o(log n) bits.',citation='streaming2019'),dict(date='2021',text='The comparison-graph paper extends stored-sample bounds and repeats the scarce-memory question.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022',text='Logarithmic memory is necessary at fixed distance for time-invariant testers with asymptotic averaged error; this model differs from the source’s streaming convention.',citation='memory2022')],status='source_open',status_note='Checked the complete 2019, 2021 and 2022 papers, including the later paper’s explicit distinction between time-varying and time-invariant updates. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution of the stated time-varying scarce-memory sample tradeoff; this is not an exhaustive current-status certification.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Probes whether an elementary distribution-testing task has a sharp storage barrier even when data is plentiful; the memory model changes that boundary. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the full scarce-regime question, ℓ₁ promise, source success probability, parameter dependence and time-varying state model. Kept the later time-invariant theorem separate and avoided extrapolating stored-sample algorithms outside their intended range.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['The task is to recognize a uniform distribution from one stream of independent samples.','The tester retains m bits, potentially fewer than needed to store one sample.','The target is the minimum sample count, including whether any finite count suffices.','The source permits time-varying state transitions, with no computation-time restriction.','A later logarithmic memory lower bound uses time-invariant updates and does not settle this model.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','streaming2019','memory2022'])),notes=['Replaced the truncated excerpt with the complete sample-complexity target.','Defined the distribution promise, 3/4 success probability, all parameters and persistent-state model.','Recovered the time-varying convention from the original and later papers.','Separated the later time-invariant lower bound and the stored-sample parameter caveat.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
